\paragraph{CoNLL NER free-form results.}
Extending the free-form ablation to CoNLL NER (Qwen3-8B, 4 seeds, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$), LP range median is $0.058{\pm}0.007$ across seeds, higher than SciERC ($0.041$) but still compressed given CoNLL's 86.8\% tied fraction under constrained decoding.
Free-form decoding reduces the tied fraction modestly, yet selection remains below single-sample: LP-selected F1${=}0.896$ vs.\ single-sample $0.904$ ($\Delta{=}{-}0.77$~pp).
Quality estimation under free-form decoding yields SJ $\rho{=}0.461$, EM $\rho{=}0.471$, LP $\rho{=}0.326$, comparable to constrained results (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}).
The failure to improve selection despite reduced compression is consistent with CoNLL's low oracle headroom (4.0~pp at $N{=}8$), which limits the selection space regardless of score distribution quality.

\section{LP Selection Failure Stability}\label{app:lp_failure_stability}

We analyze RE LP selection failures across three random seeds (constrained decoding, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B) to assess whether failures are systematic or stochastic.

\paragraph{Pairwise overlap.}
LP selection failures (instances where LP-selected F1 $<$ single-sample F1) show moderate cross-seed stability: the average pairwise Jaccard similarity is 0.42 (range: 0.38--0.46); failures are partially systematic (attributable to instance complexity) but not fully deterministic.
The 3-way intersection contains 34 instances (27.9\% of any-seed failures), which constitutes a stable failure core.

\paragraph{Instance complexity co-occurs with failure.}
Table~\ref{tab:stable_failures} stratifies instances by failure stability.
Stable failures (all 3 seeds) involve more gold relations (2.12 vs.\ 1.62 for never-fail instances) and higher single-sample F1 (0.688 vs.\ 0.429); LP mis-selects on instances where the model already performs well, precisely the instances where selection would be most valuable.
Failure rate increases monotonically with gold relation count: 4.3\% at 1 relation, 20.7\% at 3 relations, 26.2\% at 6 relations.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{RE LP selection failure stability across 3 seeds (SciERC, $N{=}8$). Stable failures concentrate on relation-dense instances with high single-sample F1.}
\label{tab:stable_failures}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Group & $n$ & Gold rels & Greedy F1 & LP $\Delta$F1 \\
\midrule
Stable fail (3 seeds) & 34 & 2.12 & 0.688 & $-$0.499 \\
Fail 2+ seeds & 26 & 2.46 & 0.774 & $-$0.450 \\
Fail 1 seed & 62 & 2.32 & 0.692 & $-$0.239 \\
Never fail & 429 & 1.62 & 0.429 & $+$0.041 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Flip instances.}
Only 5 RE instances with gold relations flip from LP failure (constrained) to LP success (free-form), with mean gold relation count 3.67 (well above the population mean of 1.86).
These instances show large constrained LP errors ($\Delta{=}{-}0.430$) that reverse under free-form decoding ($\Delta{=}{+}0.503$), but their rarity (5/397) confirms that the RE selection flip is attributable to aggregate variance reallocation rather than systematic instance-level correction.

